DOC for DUMPDIR v1.10 of 05/11/84

DUMPDIR was styled after North Star CP/M's DIRDUMP, but is a totally
new program. It will work properly in any CP/M 2.2 system. In CP/M plus
systems, the program may falsely assume that the displayed information is
coming from a floppy when it really accesses a hard disk.

Syntax:

A>DUMPDIR [d:]afn[ oo]

(The ambiguous filename MUST be supplied.)


DUMPDIR will display the following information:

- whether the disk is hard or floppy
- the maximum number of directory entries
- the number of directory entries displayed (if >0)
- the number of free directory entries 				v1.10
- the group allocations for all directory entries, sorted
  alphabetically by filename unless [N]osort option specified	v1.10
- option to display all directory entries [A]ll			v1.10

A typical display may look like:

A>dumpdir dump*.*

DUMPDIR vX.XX by ESKAY  xx/xx/84

Hard drive directory:
Capacity:  512 entries,   4 entries displayed,  218 entries free.

E00:DUMPDIR .COM   0322
E00:DUMPDIR .MAC   0255 0277
E00:DUMPIT  .TST   0333 0343 0355 0356 0356 0358 0359 035A
E00:DUMPIT  .TST   035B 035C 035D

The above information indicates the allocation group numbers assigned
to the filenames on the left. Note that each line can only display
data for one directory entry. Therefore, as in the case of DUMPIT.TST,
multiple directory entries are displayed. The group numbers displayed
are the same numbers you would use under DU's "G" command. So, to use DU
to display the first sector of the file DUMPDIR.MAC you would enter:
A>du g255

Smaller disk units and formats use 8-bit group allocation numbers
(disks with a total of less than 256 disk blocks such as 8" SSSD). In those
cases, 16 group numbers of 8 bits each are displayed per filename.


OPTIONS								v1.10

Currently, two options are supported:

N - option (Nosort):
	The N option displays the directory as it is on disk rather
	than sorting it before display.
A - option (All):
	The A option displays ALL directory entries on the current
	drive.
A and N options can be combined. Example:

A>dumpdir *.* a		;show all entries
A>dumpdir e:x a		;same (filename irrelevant but necessary)
A>dumpdir *.mac n	;show all MAC files, unsorted
A>dumpdir *.* an	;show everything, unsorted
A>dumpdir *.* na	;same

NOTE:
The A option also displays erased directory entries. These entries will
be displayed with "**" in place of the user number. In a sorted
display, they will always be last.
